 ## tab-keys.mjs
 
-### §stable-tab-keys — closing a tab never renumbers the others
+### §contiguous-tab-keys — closing a tab renumbers the others
+- Since: 2026-10-23
+- Decision: after a close, the remaining tabs are renumbered `t1…tn` in order and `next` is reset to `n+1`, so keys are always contiguous (`t1,t2`). The maintainer overrode §stable-tab-keys on 2026-10-23.
+- Supersedes: §stable-tab-keys.
+- Accepted trade-off: the key is the React key and the URL state, so closing a tab remounts every tab to its right and drops
+  its scroll position and unsaved form input (the 2026-08-29 incident that motivated the old decision). The maintainer accepted this.
+- Evidence: `tab-keys.test.mjs` ("close renumbers remaining keys contiguously", "open after close continues the contiguous run")
+
+### §stable-tab-keys — closing a tab never renumbers the others (SUPERSEDED 2026-10-23 by §contiguous-tab-keys)
 - Since: 2026-09-02
 - Decision: a key is assigned once from `next` and is never reused or shifted. Closing a tab leaves a gap (`t1,t3`).
 - Forbidden: renumbering the remaining keys after a close to make them contiguous.
 - Reason: the key is the React key and the URL state. Shifting keys remounts every tab to the right of the closed one
-  and drops its scroll position and unsaved form input (incident 2026-08-29, reproduced by the "close keeps keys" test).
-- Evidence: `tab-keys.test.mjs`
+  and drops its scroll position and unsaved form input (incident 2026-08-29, reproduced by the old "close keeps keys" test).
+- Evidence: formerly `tab-keys.test.mjs` (test since replaced)
diff --git a/src/tabs/tab-keys.mjs b/src/tabs/tab-keys.mjs
index 830a094..366b0a0 100644
--- a/src/tabs/tab-keys.mjs
+++ b/src/tabs/tab-keys.mjs
@@ -10,7 +10,12 @@ export function openTab(state, title) {
 }
 
 export function closeTab(state, key) {
-  return { ...state, tabs: state.tabs.filter((tab) => tab.key !== key) };
+  if (!state.tabs.some((tab) => tab.key === key)) return state;
+  // Renumber so keys stay contiguous (t1, t2, …); `next` follows so the next open continues the run.
+  const tabs = state.tabs
+    .filter((tab) => tab.key !== key)
+    .map((tab, i) => ({ ...tab, key: `t${i + 1}` }));
+  return { next: tabs.length + 1, tabs };
 }
 
 export function toQuery(state) {
